/**
 * The core guard's rules: which paths of a copy are kit core, and what to reach for instead of
 * editing them. Read by `scripts/kit-guard.mjs` through `lib/kit-guard-lib.mjs`. Kit-owned and
 * ported by `kit:upgrade` like any other `scripts/lib/` file, so the map stays true as the kit's
 * seams grow.
 *
 * Three tiers, first match wins, in the order `sealed` → `open` → `steer`; a path no rule names
 * is open:
 *
 * - `sealed` — generated files. The one deny: editing one by hand is undone by the next run of
 *   its generator, so the guard names the command (`regenerate`) instead.
 * - `open` — no friction: the examples, Home and its registries, the theme tokens and the logo,
 *   the Login image, the design docs, and anything that is the app's own.
 * - `steer` — the core core. The first edit to one in a session is paused once (refused, with
 *   the seam that usually serves better) and the retry goes through; a `localEdits` entry in
 *   `.rocketflare.json` records it if the edit stands — that entry is what `/rp-upgrade` shows
 *   when a later kit release changes the same lines.
 *
 * Every `instead` names a seam that exists in this kit: a member of `ServerPlugin` / `UiPlugin` /
 * `SharedPlugin` (`apps/web/src/plugins/types.ts`, `packages/shared/src/plugins/types.ts`), an
 * export of `@/plugins/api`, `@/db/schema/kit`, a `Platform` member (`apps/web/src/platform/types.ts`),
 * or a documented config var or command. Where there is no seam the rule says so, rather than
 * inventing one. Rocketflare's Worker rules (the wrangler types, the Worker entry, the
 * `worker-exports.ts` barrel) have no file here; the Node runtime (`src/node/**`) and the
 * platform seam take their place.
 */

/** @type {import('./kit-guard-rules.d.mts').GuardRule[]} */
